===Etymology 2===
From {{bor|en|ga|-}} and {{bor|en|gd|-}} {{m|ga|tuig|t=to understand}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|colloquial|regional}} To [[realise]] something; to [[catch on]]; to [[recognize]] someone or something.
#: {{ux|en|He hasn't '''twigged''' that we're planning a surprise party for him.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1765|chapter=A Song in High Life|title=The Merry Medley|volume=1|location=London|publisher=W. Hoggard|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8qaqE96UeY4C&pg=PA35|page=35|passage=I pray you now send me some dub, / A bottle or two to the needy. / I beg you won't bring it yourself, / The harman is at the Old-Bailey; / I'd rather you'd send it behalf, / For, if they '''twig''' you they'll nail you.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1915|chapter=Putting on the Screw|title=Caught in the Net|by=w:Émile Gaboriau|original=Les Esclaves de Paris|location=New York|publisher=Charles Scribner's Sons|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/caughtinnet00gabo/page/22/|page=23|passage=I '''twigged''' him at once, by the description you gave me. I never see a cove togged out as he was,—tall hat, light sit-down-upons, and a short coat—wasn't it cut short! but in really bang-up style.|translation=J'y ai '''reconnu''' le particulier que vous m'avez dit. Bien vêtu, ma foi! Chapeau rogné, tout plat, pantalon clair, en fourreau de parapluie, veston court, oh! mais d'un court... enfin, le dernier genre. }}
#* {{quote-web| 1=en| date=2012-05-30| author=John E. McIntyre| title=A future for copy editors| site=Baltimore Sun| url=http://www.baltimoresun.com/news/language-blog/bal-a-future-for-copy-editors-20120530,0,4576028.story?track=rss| passage=Well, with fewer people doing two or three times the work, you may have already '''twigged''' to this.| accessdate=2 June 2012| archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130623045833/http://www.baltimoresun.com/news/language-blog/bal-a-future-for-copy-editors-20120530,0,4576028.story?track=rss| archivedate=23 June 2013}}
#* {{quote-av|en|author=Drachinifel|title=The Russian 2nd Pacific Squadron - Voyage of the Damned|date=13 March 2019|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9Mdi_Fh9_Ag|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230129152141/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9Mdi_Fh9_Ag|archivedate=29 January 2023|accessdate=1 February 2023|time=18:23|text=At this point in our story, we first meet the fleet repair ship ''Kamchatka'', who everyone would soon become regrettably familiar with.  Her entry into our account here was her signal that she was under attack by torpedo boats!  When she was asked how many, she replied "about eight, from all directions!".  ''Eventually'', someone '''twigged''' to the fact that absolutely nobody ''else'' could see so much as a seagull, let alone any exceptionally-lost Japanese torpedo boats.  And, when nothing actually happened, ''Kamchatka'' refused to say that it was a false alarm, only that it had altered course and the torpedo boats had gone away.}}
# To [[understand]] the meaning of (a person); to [[comprehend]].
#: {{ux|en|Do you '''twig''' me?}}
# To [[observe]] [[slyly]]; also, to [[perceive]]; to [[discover]].<ref name=Webster>{{R:Webster 1913}}</ref>
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1763|author=w:Samuel Foote|title=w:The Mayor of Garratt|section=act 2|passage=Now '''twig''' him; now mind him: mark how he hawls his muscles about.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1863|author=w:Nathaniel Hawthorne|title=[[gutenberg:8090|Our Old Home]]|passage=This excellent man appears to have sunk into himself in a sitting posture, {{...|with a thin leg crossed over his knee, a book in one hand, and a finger of the other under his chin, I believe, or applied to the side of his nose, or to some equally familiar purpose;}} while his exceedingly homely and wrinkled face, held a little on one side, twinkles at you with the shrewdest complacency, as if he were looking right into your eyes and '''twigged''' something there which you had half a mind to conceal from him.}}

